g-lede-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The $1.9 Trillion Question in the Senate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2021-03-01T11:21:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'NYTNow', 'section_name': 'Briefing', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/10abb506-e2ec-5d56-8f27-0ac63b868ac0', 'word_count': 1558, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10abb506-e2ec-5d56-8f27-0ac63b868ac0'}, {'abstract': 'President Xi Jinping may be putting personal ambition ahead of his country’s best interests.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/01/opinion/hong-kong-security-law.html', 'snippet': 'President Xi Jinping may be putting personal ambition ahead of his country’s best interests.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sunday, the Hong Kong authorities charged 47 pro-democracy activists with “conspiracy to commit subversion” against the Chinese government under the national security law it imposed on the city last summer. Beijing must be happy with the catch, which elegantly nets under a single accusation both advocates of outright independence for Hong Kong and the city’s old-school loyal opposition. The People’s Republic of China is safe now. Glory to its leader.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/01/opinion/01lian/merlin_182018523_7b3808bd-24bc-4d31-a28d-d9434c373d12-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/01/opinion/01lian/merlin_18