inal': 'By Charles Yu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d7a12c9-68f5-560c-8394-7b7e01fb2d18', 'word_count': 944, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d7a12c9-68f5-560c-8394-7b7e01fb2d18'}, {'abstract': 'A conversation about strong recent debuts, and how canon and community can be in tension.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/02/arts/music/popcast-jazz.html', 'snippet': 'A conversation about strong recent debuts, and how canon and community can be in tension.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Plenty of genres have anxiety about their relationships to history, but perhaps none more loudly than jazz. The conversation about forward movement vs. reverence of the past is at play constantly, and motivates some of the last year’s most exciting releases by Immanuel Wilkins, Jyoti, Exploding Star Orchestra, even the Sun Ra Arkestra.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/02/arts/02popcast/02popcast-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 438, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/02/arts/02popcast/02popcast-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 438}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/02/arts/02popcast/02popcast-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 747, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/02/arts/02popcast/02popcast-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1493, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'imag