d', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Winnie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/10a52508-6a16-55cd-926e-fd90a85e1228', 'word_count': 1532, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10a52508-6a16-55cd-926e-fd90a85e1228'}, {'abstract': 'Many of the country’s finest antiquities were stolen under cover of war, ending up in elite museums all over the globe. Should they be returned?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/04/magazine/ghazni-panels-afghanistan-art.html', 'snippet': 'Many of the country’s finest antiquities were stolen under cover of war, ending up in elite museums all over the globe. Should they be returned?', 'lead_paragraph': 'To hear more audio stories from publishers like The New York Times, download Audm for iPhone or Android.In November 2013, Nora von Achenbach, curator at the Museum of Arts and Crafts in Hamburg, Germany, examined the catalog for an upcoming auction by the Paris-based dealer Boisgirard-Antonini. The glossy pages offered a bevy of antiquities for sale: bronze figurines, jewelry and a statue from ancient Egypt estimated at more than 300,000 euros, or almost half a million dollars. But von Achenbach was interested in a pale marble tablet, carved with arabesques, vines and Persian script. Lot 104, an “important epigraphic panel with interlacings from the palace of Mas’ud III,” was dated to the 12th century, from the capital of the Ghaznavid Empire, in what is today Afghanistan.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/07/magazine/07mag-antiquities-promo/07mag-antiquities-promo-articleLarge-v5.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/07/magazine/07mag-antiquities-promo/07mag-antiquities-promo-articleLarge-v5.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/07/magazine/07mag-antiquities-promo/07mag-antiquities-promo-jumbo-v5.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/07/magazine/07mag-antiquities-promo/07mag-antiquities-promo-superJumbo-v5.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/07/magazine/07mag-antiquities-promo/07mag-antiquities-promo-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/03/07/magazine/07mag-antiquities-promo/07mag-antiquities-promo-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/07/magazine/07mag-antiquities-promo/07mag-antiquities-promo-thumbLarge-v5.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How One Looted Artifact Tells the Story of Modern Afghanistan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': ' Written in Marble', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Arts and Antiquities Looting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Destruction of Cultural Antiquities and Heritage', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Museum', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Museum of Afghanistan', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-04T10:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthieu Aikins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthieu', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Aikins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8567d73d-afd9-5edb-927c-af52812d657a', 'word_count': 7130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8567d73d-afd9-5edb-927c-af52812d657a'}, {'abstract': 'When pandemic New York seemed at its most surreal, the park, with its abundant wildlife and familiar progression of the seasons, offered a vision of normal life to a book critic who wandered it daily.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/04/travel/finding-refuge-and-a-snowy-owl-in-central-park.html', 'snippet': 'When pandemic New York seemed at its most surreal, the park, with its abundant wildlife and familiar progression of the seasons, offered a vision of normal life to a book critic who wandered it daily.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was a chilly Saturday night in February and there was more than a foot of snow in Central Park, along with slippery patches of black ice and slushy, calf-high puddles. But some 200 New Yorkers carefully made their way to the Reservoir in hopes of catching a glimpse of the magical snowy owl, who had touched down in the park the week before in what was reported as the first visit there by the species in more than 130 years.', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank