n Calls Out Her Main Foe: Sexism', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quote of the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2021-03-05T05:14:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Today’s Paper', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3a8ba2d-93f1-5616-8bca-122741527672', 'word_count': 47, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3a8ba2d-93f1-5616-8bca-122741527672'}, {'abstract': 'The country’s culture of argument has come under the sway of a more ideological, more identity-focused model imported from the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/05/opinion/french-intellectual-life-america.html', 'snippet': 'The country’s culture of argument has come under the sway of a more ideological, more identity-focused model imported from the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the end of last summer, Le Débat, France’s most prestigious intellectual review, accompanied its 40th-anniversary issue with a wholly unexpected announcement: It would cease publication forthwith. Le Débat and its three or four thousand loyal readers had maintained an allegiance to the political left since the Cold War — but the meaning of “left” has been shifting. Rivals now claim the term, particularly social movements that arose in France in the 1980s to champion what is variously called identity politics or social justice. After waging a decades-long twilight struggle against these movements, Le Débat has lost.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/05/opinion/05caldwell/05caldwell-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 780, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/05/opinion/05caldwell/05caldwell-articleLarge-v2.