aving to be self-reliant when the U.S. government fails to step up and provide support.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Covid-19 Devastated Many Communities. But Not Cherokee Nation.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cherokee Indians', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bureau of Indian Affairs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oklahoma', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mankiller, Wilma', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'your-feed-opinionvideo', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-10T10:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/72bae333-c41c-50ac-ba95-84dcc0aea01e', 'word_count': 106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72bae333-c41c-50ac-ba95-84dcc0aea01e'}, {'abstract': 'A child benefit plan in President Biden’s $1.9 trillion pandemic relief bill would give monthly checks to families with children. Do you think this plan should become permanent?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/10/learning/should-the-government-provide-a-guaranteed-income-for-families-with-children.html', 'snippet': 'A child benefit plan in President Biden’s $1.9 trillion pandemic relief bill would give monthly checks to families with children. Do you think this plan should become permanent?', 'lead_paragraph': 'According to the government definition of poverty, about 10 million children in the United States fall under this category. Does that surprise you? How does knowing this make you feel?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/05/us/politics/00dc-childtax3-HPsub/merlin_184627872_26935ec1-534e-4f29-ad1e-0c3c68000c2d-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/05/us/politics/00dc-childtax3-HPsub/merlin_184627872_26935ec1-534e-4f29-ad1e-0c3c68000c2d-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/05/us/politics/00dc-childtax3-HPsub/merlin_184627872_26935ec1-534e-4f29-ad1e-0c3c68000c2d-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/05/us/politics/00dc-childtax3-HPsub/merlin_184627872_26935ec1-534e-4f29-ad1e-0c3c68000c2d-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/05/us/politics/00dc-childtax3-HPsub/00dc-childtax3-HPsub-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/03/05/us/politics/00dc-childtax3-HPsub/00dc-childtax3-HPsub-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/05/us/politics/00dc-childtax3-HPsub/00dc-childtax3-HPsub-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Should the Government Provide a Guaranteed Income for Families With Children?', 'kicker': 'student opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2021-03-10T10:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole Daniels', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Daniels', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7418831e-ddd8-5376-abf8-82d8b30e86df', 'word_count': 1224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7418831e-ddd8-5376-abf8-82d8b30e86df'}, {'abstract': 'The I.R.S. is often unable to detect or fight blatant tax cheating by the rich and big businesses. Restoring the missing revenue is a solution that pays for itself.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/10/opinion/deficits-taxes-biden-infrastructure.html', 'snippet': 'The I.R.S. is often unable to detect or fight blatant tax cheating by the rich and big businesses. Restoring the missing revenue is a solution that pays for itself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the passage of a deficit-financed $1.9 trillion relief bill just hours away, Democrats in Congress may soon pivot to new agenda items, including President Biden’s Build Back Better plan for infrastructure and other critical investments. And those lawmakers will inevitably face intense pressure from fiscal moderates to include tax “pay fors” in spending legislation. One of the best ways to raise plenty of revenue — and help honest taxpayers — is to effectively battle tax cheats. To do that, though, Mr. Biden and Congress must seize the chance to revamp and restore the federal government’s own infrastructure: the Internal Revenue Service.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 