By Chye-Ching Huang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chye-ching', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Huang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/77b57e29-bb05-539f-b581-f343906f1cbc', 'word_count': 1057, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77b57e29-bb05-539f-b581-f343906f1cbc'}, {'abstract': 'It’s a trillion-dollar crisis that’s crippling the economy and dooming millions of college graduates. So what’s the fairest way to fix it?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/10/opinion/biden-student-loans-cancellation.html', 'snippet': 'It’s a trillion-dollar crisis that’s crippling the economy and dooming millions of college graduates. So what’s the fairest way to fix it?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The problem of student loan debt has reached crisis proportions. As a college degree has grown increasingly necessary for economic mobility, so has the $1.7 trillion in student loan debt that Americans have taken on to access that opportunity. President Biden has put some debt cancellation on the table, but progressive Democrats are pushing him for more. So what is the fairest way to correct course?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cancel America’s Student Loan Debt! But How?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Student Loans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Biden, Joseph R Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal-State Relations (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Urban Institute', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taylor, Astra', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baum, Sandy', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-10T10:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Coaston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Coaston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b0928ea-626d-5326-8b3d-0fcb05e5ed73', 'word_count': 347, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b0928ea-626d-5326-8b3d-0fcb05e5ed73'}, {'abstract': 'For years we’ve been crediting endorphins, but it’s really about the endocannabinoids.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/10/well/move/running-exercise-mental-effects.html', 'snippet': 'For years we’ve been crediting endorphins, but it’s really about the endocannabinoids.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We can stop crediting endorphins, the natural opioid painkillers produced by our bodies, for the floaty euphoria we often feel during aerobic exercise, according to a nifty new study of men, women and treadmills. In the study, runners developed a gentle intoxication, known as a runner’s high, even if researchers had blocked their bodies’ ability to respond to endorphins, suggesting that those substances could not be behind the buzz. Instead, the study suggests, a different set of biochemicals resembling internally homegrown versions of cannabis, better known as marijuana, are likely to be responsible.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/08/well/00well-physed-runners-high/merlin_171781443_a262626f-9a3f-4f69-a86f-465875f8786f-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/08/well/00well-physed-runners-high/merlin_171781443_a262626f-9a3f-4f69-a86f-465875f8786f-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/08/well/00well-physed-runners-high/merlin_171781443_a262626f-9a3f-4f69-a86f-465875f8786f-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/08/well/00well-physed-runners-high/merlin_171781443_a262626f-9a3f-4f69-a86f-465875f8786f-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/08/well/00well-physed-runners-high/00well-physed-runners-high-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/03/08/well/00well-physed-runners-high/00well-physed-runners-high-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/08/well/00well-physed-runners-high/00well-physed-runners-high-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting to the Bottom of the Runner’s High', 'kicker': 'Phys Ed', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endorphins', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marijuana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Anxiety and Stress', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Running', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Psychoneuroendocrinology (Journal)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-10T10:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Well', 'section_name': 'Well', 'subsection_name': 'Move', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gretchen Reynolds', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gretchen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reynolds', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1cf86e9-7a63-5ae0-90da-902b551ceba1', 'word_count': 875, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1cf86e9-7a63-5ae0-90da-902b551ceba1'}, {'abstract': 'Videoconferencing is good enough to replace a lot of pointless business travel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/10/opinion/business-travel-videoconferencing.html', 'snippet': 'Videoconferencing is good enough to replace a lot of pointless business travel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I once flew round-trip from San Francisco to London to participate in an hourlong discussion about a book. Another time it was San Francisco-Hong Kong, Hong Kong-Singapore and back again for two lunch meetings, each more lunch than meeting. I went to Atlanta once to interview an official who flaked out at the last minute. And there was that time in Miami: three days, 5,000 miles, hotel, rental car — and on the way back a sinking realization that the person I’d gone to profile was too dull for a profile.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/10/opinion/10manjoo-lead/10manjoo-lead-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/10/opinion/10manjoo-lead/10manjoo-lead-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',