e, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Dominique', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'James', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}, {'firstname': 'Hannah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nyhart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 4}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/245aa6c6-e3ad-51f3-8554-ff12e98c7789', 'word_count': 1081, 'uri': 'nyt://article/245aa6c6-e3ad-51f3-8554-ff12e98c7789'}, {'abstract': 'The past year has seen progressive pipe dreams become reality. But can we hold on to what we’ve learned?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/11/opinion/covid-eviction-prison-internet-policy.html', 'snippet': 'The past year has seen progressive pipe dreams become reality. But can we hold on to what we’ve learned?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last spring, as a poorly understood virus swept the planet\xad, something remarkable happened: Across the country, all levels of government put in place policies that just a few months earlier would have been seen by most people — not to mention most politicians — as radical and politically naïve.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/11/opinion/11covid-cohen-promo/11covid-cohen-promo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/11/opinion/11covid-cohen-promo/11covid-cohen-promo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/11/opinion/11covid-cohen-promo/11covid-cohen-promo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtyp