000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel M. Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/08aa0a18-80b3-5522-9a16-bb3f0bd6c825', 'word_count': 1565, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08aa0a18-80b3-5522-9a16-bb3f0bd6c825'}, {'abstract': 'Classifying a rare blood disorder as a cancer opened new doors for disease investigation, treatment and hope for a cure.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/11/well/live/cancer-blood.html', 'snippet': 'Classifying a rare blood disorder as a cancer opened new doors for disease investigation, treatment and hope for a cure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I have a rare blood disorder. When it was diagnosed 25 years ago, it was called an “orphan disease,” meaning the small number of people affected didn’t justify research investments by major pharmaceutical companies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/06/12/well/00well-cancer1/merlin_156360894_a4e5793b-b62d-45ef-b20e-740e16dfad50-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/06/12/well/00well-cancer1/merlin_156360894_a4e5793b-b62d-45ef-b20e-740e16dfad50-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/06/12/well/00well-cancer1/merlin_156360894_a4e5793b-b62d-45ef-b20e-740e16dfad50-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/06/12/well/00well-cancer1/merlin_156360894_a4e5793b-b62d-45ef-b20e-740e16dfad50-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/06/12/well/00well-cancer1/00well-cancer1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/06/12/well/00well-cancer1/00well-cancer1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/06/12/well/00well-cancer1/00well-cancer1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nobody Wants Cancer. But a ‘Big C’ Label Has Surprising Upsides.', 'kicker': 'Voices', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Clinical Trials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bone Marrow', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blood', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-11T10:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Well', 'section_name': 'Well', 'subsection_name': 'Live', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ruth Fein Revell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ruth', 'middlename': 'Fein', 'lastname': 'Revell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45815c23-2962-55e8-8480-43a289baf000', 'word_count': 928, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45815c23-2962-55e8-8480-43a289baf000'}, {'abstract': 'The director of “Da 5 Bloods” talks about why his old movies still resonate, whether an awards snub even matters and how it’s not his job to end racism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/11/opinion/sway-kara-swisher-spike-lee.html', 'snippet': 'The director of “Da 5 Bloods” talks about why his old movies still resonate, whether an awards snub even matters and how it’s not his job to end racism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Spike Lee has a knack for seeing what’s ahead. After Eric Garner’s death in 2014 and George Floyd’s death last year, Mr. Lee’s 1989 film “Do the Right Thing,” which culminates in a violent confrontation between a Black character and a white police officer, was seen as downright prophetic.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/12/opinion/11sway-lee-image/11sway-lee-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/12/opinion/11sway-lee-image/11sway-lee-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/12/opinion/11sway-lee-image/11sway-lee-image-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/12/opinion/11sway-lee-image/11sway-lee-image-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1619, 'width': 1619, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/12/opinion/11sway-lee-image/11sway-lee-image-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/03/12/opinion/11sway-lee-image/11sway-lee-image-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/12/opinion/11sway-lee-image/11sway-lee-image-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Spike Lee Predicts the Future', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Spike', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Da 5 Bloods (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Do the Right Thing (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'BlacKkKlansman (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golden Globes (Awards)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Netflix Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-11T10:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kara Swisher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Swisher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/468ec69b-ff61-5c89-ba7c-5573c8328dfa', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/468ec69b-ff61-5c89-ba7c-5573c8328dfa'}, {'abstract': 'Yearning for the Before Times as a mythic era risks obscuring the ways in which the Before was really many different kinds of before.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/11/opinion/covid-isolation-narrative.html', 'snippet': 'Yearning for the Before Times as a mythic era risks obscuring the ways in which the Before was really many different kinds of before.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last March, during the two and a half weeks I spent in quarantine — sick with a mild case of Covid-19 and sequestered with my young daughter — I religiously marked off the days until it would be safe for us to leave the apartment, daydreaming constantly about what it would be like: walking on the sidewalk beneath bare tree branches rattling in the wind, skin striped by cold sunlight, watching my toddler point at the purple door she loved across the street. But once the day of our freedom came, I delayed another day, and then another day after that. At the time, I told myself it was caution — wanting to make doubly, triply sure we were no longer infectious — but I think I was also loath to give up the horizon itself, the end of our sequestering. Some part of me knew I wouldn’t get another ending for a while.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/11/opinion/11covid-jamison-promo/11covid-jamison-promo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/11/opinion/11covid-jamison-promo/11covid-jamison-promo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/11/opinion/11covid-jamison-promo/11covid-jamison-promo-jumbo.jpg', 'height'