tion_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1ed74e6-9932-5a9e-8b21-75dc1e345121', 'word_count': 680, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1ed74e6-9932-5a9e-8b21-75dc1e345121'}, {'abstract': 'Pitino got used to making runs to the N.C.A.A. tournament with big-name programs, until Louisville fired him in 2017. Iona gave him a chance to find another way in.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/13/sports/ncaabasketball/rick-pitino-iona-ncaa-tournament.html', 'snippet': 'Pitino got used to making runs to the N.C.A.A. tournament with big-name programs, until Louisville fired him in 2017. Iona gave him a chance to find another way in.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ATLANTIC CITY, N.J. — The old Atlantic City Boardwalk Hall, with its Renaissance-style architecture, its grand arched roof and the world’s largest pipe organ, is a fitting platform for a final act. And so, there was Rick Pitino late Saturday afternoon, with only family members and friends in the cavernous arena, stalking the sidelines in a dapper navy suit, waving his rolled-up notes around like a maestro conducting an orchestra.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/14/sports/13maac-iona-print/merlin_184996977_f01064fc-2965-4859-808c-b1c2e64d3e05-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/14/sports/13maac-iona-print/merlin_184996977_f01064fc-2965-4859-808c-b1c2e64d3e05-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': '