ople around the world to adapt to their spaces and exist within the confines of four walls. We asked readers how their relationship to their homes was affected.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/13/at-home/pandemic-home.html', 'snippet': 'Lockdowns and restrictions have forced millions of people around the world to adapt to their spaces and exist within the confines of four walls. We asked readers how their relationship to their homes was affected.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before the pandemic began, your home may have merely been a place to sleep. Or maybe it was a precious escape from the hustle and bustle of the outside world. But now, a year after the World Health Organization declared the coronavirus outbreak a pandemic, home has taken on an entirely new meaning. A year of lockdowns and various restrictions have forced millions of people around the world to adapt to their spaces and exist within the confines of four walls. Many could no longer afford to pay rent and subsequently lost their homes, their sense of security.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/14/multimedia/14ah-anniversary-readers1/14ah-anniversary-readers1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/14/multimedia/14ah-anniversary-readers1/14ah-anniversary-readers1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 480}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/14/multimedia/14ah-anniversary-readers1/14ah-anniversary-readers1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 714, 'width': 892, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/14/multimedia/14ah-anniversary-readers1/14ah-anniversary-readers1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 714, 'width': 892, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/14/multimedia/14ah-anniversary-readers1/14ah-anniversary-readers1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/03/14/multimedia/14ah-anniversary-readers1/14ah-anniversary-readers1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/14/multimedia/14ah-anniversary-readers1/14ah-anniversary-readers1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How the Pandemic Has Transformed the Idea of Home', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Has a Pandemic Year Changed Your Idea of ‘Home’?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarantine (Life and Culture)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarantines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Epidemics', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Telecommuting', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Loneliness', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-14T04:19:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'AtHome', 'section_name': 'At Home', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sara Aridi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Aridi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/07a3f4e0-c648-5660-bd31-4a7d72796b0c', 'word_count': 2060, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07a3f4e0-c648-5660-bd31-4a7d72796b0c'}, {'abstract': 'Once a limited-capacity event, now a writer conversation can be enjoyed from the exclusivity of your living room.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/13/at-home/book-tour-in-your-living-room.html', 'snippet': 'Once a limited-capacity event, now a writer conversation can be enjoyed from the exclusivity of your living room.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Authors releasing books in the past year have experienced a radically altered literary landscape — perhaps most significantly, they have lost the ability to venture out on the standard book tour, which typically includes a series of readings and talks across the country. While many book lovers yearn for the days of big-name events or cozy chats at indie bookstores, others are enjoying access for the first time. For those who do not live in the major cities and college towns that tend to be destinations for top writers, the pandemic has offered them the ability to experience these events online.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/14/multimedia/14AH-ONLINEAUTHORS-COMBO/14ah-onlineauthors-combo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/14/multimedia/14AH-ONLINEAUTHORS-COMBO/14ah-onlineauthors-combo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/14/multimedia/14AH-ONLINEAUTHORS-COMBO/14ah-onlineauthors-combo-jumbo.j