k', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Haag', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haag', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df9ae3f0-daba-5370-8ef3-0314cf0d4889', 'word_count': 1373, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df9ae3f0-daba-5370-8ef3-0314cf0d4889'}, {'abstract': 'The S.E.C. is making E.S.G. a priority.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/15/business/dealbook/sec-esg-priority.html', 'snippet': 'The S.E.C. is making E.S.G. a priority.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Allison Herren Lee was named acting chair of the Securities and Exchange Commission in January, and since then she has been active, especially when it comes to environmental, social and governance, or E.S.G., issues. The agency has issued a flurry of notices that such disclosures will be priorities this year. Today, Ms. Lee, who was appointed as a commissioner by President Donald Trump in 2019, is speaking at the Center for American Progress, where she will call for input on additional E.S.G. transparency, according to prepared remarks seen by DealBook.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/15/business/15db-newsletter-sec-esg1/15db-newsletter-sec-esg1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/15/business/15db-newsletter-sec-esg1/15db-newsletter-sec-esg1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/15/business/15db-newsletter-sec-esg1/15db-newsletter-sec-esg1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type':