ned up than it needs. It should be distributing them to countries that have few to none.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Finally, the United States appears to have an enviable record in one aspect of the pandemic.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/18/opinion/18tufekci/18tufekci-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 732, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/18/opinion/18tufekci/18tufekci-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 732}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/18/opinion/18tufekci/18tufekci-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 840, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/18/opinion/18tufekci/18tufekci-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1680, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/18/opinion/18tufekci/18tufekci-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/03/18/opinion/18tufekci/18tufekci-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/18/opinion/18tufekci/18tufekci-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Let Our Doses Go!', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Embarrassment of Vaccine Riches', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AstraZeneca PLC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Food and Drug Administration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-17T09:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zeynep Tufekci', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zeynep', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tufekci', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf6be7eb-e30d-5cd5-8a2c-61262df4282e', 'word_count': 1349, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf6be7eb-e30d-5cd5-8a2c-61262df4282e'}, {'abstract': 'Health care workers are crucial to our society, yet we reward them with low wages and dangerous conditions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/17/opinion/health-care-jobs.html', 'snippet': 'Health care workers are crucial to our society, yet we reward them with low wages and dangerous conditions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Congress and the White House, policymakers are developing a bill that is expected to pour trillions into updating American transit, energy and utility systems. Repairs are certainly necessary, and it’s impossible to overstate the urgency of such a program in terms of the transition to a more sustainable economy.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/17/opinion/17Winant/17Winant-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/17/opinion/17Winant/17Winant-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/17/opinion/17Winant/17Winant-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/17/opinion/17Winant/17Winant-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/17/opinion/17Winant/17Winant-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/03/17/opinion/17Winant/17Winant-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/17/opinion/17Winant/17Winant-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Manufacturing Isn’t Coming Back. Let’s Improve These Jobs Instead.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Improve Our Vital Health Care Jobs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Domestic Service', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing and Nurses', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Care', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-17T09:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gabriel Winant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gabriel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Winant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/5212d32e-1f68-5e8e-83fe-7d7c75963992', 'word_count': 1113, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5212d32e-1f68-5e8e-83fe-7d7c75963992'}, {'abstract': 'Lingering symptoms from the coronavirus may turn out to be one of the largest mass disabling events in modern history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/17/opinion/long-covid.html', 'snippet': 'Lingering symptoms from the coronavirus may turn out to be one of the largest mass disabling events in modern history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Both of us developed Covid-19 last March in New York. We didn’t know each other at the time, but we had much in common: We didn’t imagine our symptoms would last for more than a couple of weeks. We were young and otherwise healthy, and were told we should recover easily.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Long Covid Is Not Rare. It’s a Health Crisis.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chronic Condition (Health)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disabilities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disability Insurance', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-17T09:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fiona Lowenstein and Hannah Davis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fiona', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lowenstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Hannah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f49c2039-fec4-511a-9463-d4b18381178c', 'word_count': 1433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f49c2039-fec4-511a-9463-d4b18381178c'}, {'abstract': '“I think that says a great deal about how much we as a society value the written word,” a shocked librarian said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/17/nyregion/nyc-overdue-library-book.html', 'snippet': '“I think that says a great deal about how much we as a society value the written word,” a shocked librarian said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A vintage children’s hardback turned up in the mailroom of the Queens Public Library in Auburndale recently. The book was “Ol’ Paul, the Mighty Logger,” by Glen Rounds, a collection of Paul Bunyan tall tales. According to the date stamped on the borrowing card inside, it was about 23,000 days late.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/21/nyregion/21latelibrarybook2/21latelibrarybook2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/21/nyregion/21latelibrarybook2/21latelibrarybook2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/21/nyregion/21latelibrarybook2/21latelibrarybook2-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/21/nyregion/21latelibrarybook2/21latelibrarybook2-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/21/nyregion/21latelibrarybook2/21latelibrarybook2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/03/21/nyregion/21latelibrarybook2/21latelibrarybook2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/21/nyregion/21latelibrarybook2/21latelibrarybook2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'She Kept a Library Book for 63 Years. It Was Time to Return It.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s the Late Fee on a Book Checked Out in 1957?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libraries and Librarians', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Queens Borough Public Library', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-17T09:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sasha von Oldershausen', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e6feaaf-b8aa-5bce-9cc1-62883bdaf220', 'word_count': 835, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e6feaaf-b8aa-5bce-9cc1-62883bdaf220'}, {'abstract': 'An analysis of court data shows that the areas hit hardest by the virus, largely Black and Latino neighborhoods, have the most eviction cases.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/17/realestate/new-york-city-renters-evictions.html', 'snippet': 'An analysis of court data shows that the areas hit hardest by the virus, largely Black and Latino neighborhoods, have the most eviction cases.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York City landlords are seeking evictions nearly four times more often in the neighborhoods hit hardest by Covid-19 — predominantly Black and Latino communities that have borne the brunt of both health and housing crises since the virus swept the city last year, according to a new report.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/17/us/nyc-eviction-filings-map-promo-1615986347866/nyc-eviction-filings-map-promo-1615986347866-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/17/us/nyc-eviction-filings-map-promo-1615986347866/nyc-eviction-filings-map-promo-1615986347866-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/17/us/nyc-eviction-filings-map-promo-1615986347866/nyc-eviction-filings-map-promo-1615986347866-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/17/us/nyc-eviction-filings-map-promo-1615986347866/nyc-eviction-filings-map-promo-1615986347866-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1331, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/17/us/nyc-eviction-filings-map-promo-1615986347866/nyc-eviction-fili