onavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-17T09:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stefanos Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stefanos', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a103e2b0-6f2b-58bb-ba37-8e9e1304672c', 'word_count': 882, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a103e2b0-6f2b-58bb-ba37-8e9e1304672c'}, {'abstract': 'Artificial intelligence used to evaluate job candidates must not become a tool that exacerbates discrimination.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/17/opinion/ai-employment-bias-nyc.html', 'snippet': 'Artificial intelligence used to evaluate job candidates must not become a tool that exacerbates discrimination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'American democracy depends on everyone having equal access to work. But in reality, people of color, women, those with disabilities and other marginalized groups experience unemployment or underemployment at disproportionately high rates, especially amid the economic fallout of the Covid-19 pandemic. Now the use of artificial intelligence technology for hiring may exacerbate those problems and further bake bias into the hiring process.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/17/opinion/17schellmann/17schellmann-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 460, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/17/opinion/17schellmann/17schellmann-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 460}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/17/opinion/17schellmann/17schellmann-jumbo.jpg', 'he