e': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Losing a loved one to Covid has drawn some into politics.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'internal-essential', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-17T11:36:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maggie Astor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maggie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Astor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ebaf7e2-0f83-5db4-b99e-3cd41b14f60c', 'word_count': 363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ebaf7e2-0f83-5db4-b99e-3cd41b14f60c'}, {'abstract': 'A growing community of makers are creating work that reflects their identities and challenges the history of their art form in the U.K.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/17/t-magazine/black-british-ceramics.html', 'snippet': 'A growing community of makers are creating work that reflects their identities and challenges the history of their art form in the U.K.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THERE ARE FEW objects as representative of traditional British design as Staffordshire dogs. Perched in pairs on mantelpieces across the nation by the end of the 19th century, these earthenware figurines depicting gold-collared spaniels and other dogs embody both a fantasy of rarefied country life — Queen Victoria’s beloved pet Dash was a Cavalier King Charles — and the population’s conformist tastes. Indeed, the pieces were among the most sought-after designs, along with delicate floral-patterned bone-china tea sets and intricate neo-Classical jasperware vases produced by the Potteries, the group of pioneering factories, eventually numbering in the hundreds, established in the 17th century in the coal-mining towns that form the present-day city of Stoke-on-Trent. With the rise of large-scale manufacturing in the 18th century, led by innovators like Josiah Wedgwood, ceramics — a practice that for millenniums had been the province of independent artisans working in small studios — transformed into a global industry.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '76', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/17/t-magazine/17tmag-ceramicists-02/17tmag-ceramicists-02-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/17/t-magazine/17tmag-ceramicists-02/17tmag-ceramicists-02-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/17/t-magazine/17tmag-ceramicists-02/17tmag-ceramicists-02-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/17/t-magazine/17tmag-ceramicists-02/17tmag-ceramicists-02-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/17/t-magazine/17tmag-ceramicists-02/17tmag-ceramicists-02-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/03/17/t-magazine/17tmag-ceramicists-02/17tmag-ceramicists-02-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/17/t-magazine/17tmag-ceramicists-02/17tmag-ceramicists-02-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 