drew', 'lastname': 'Cuomo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Via', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Youtube', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/84dc456f-d169-5f05-856b-c779dc514fa6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/84dc456f-d169-5f05-856b-c779dc514fa6'}, {'abstract': 'Antitrust bills would dismantle Apple and Google’s monopoly over the distribution of smartphone apps and entice companies to relocate to those states.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/18/opinion/apple-google-app-monopoly.html', 'snippet': 'Antitrust bills would dismantle Apple and Google’s monopoly over the distribution of smartphone apps and entice companies to relocate to those states.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Congress continues its interrogation of monopolistic practices of technology companies, states are getting into the game. Among the first targets on their list are Apple and Google, which together command 100 percent of the market for smartphone operating systems. New bills introduced in several states would threaten the companies’ dominance and represent an opportunity for state leaders to put fairness in commerce at the center of governance.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'States Are Right to Rebel Against Big Tech', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antitrust Laws and Competition Issues', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-18T19:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Stoller and Pat Garofalo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stoller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Pat', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garofalo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f09a788-227f-5723-b15d-3aa8046070d8', 'word_count': 969, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f09a788-227f-5723-b15d-3aa8046070d8'}, {'abstract': 'Millions of Americans were forgotten when Congress extended protection for student loan holders. I’m one of them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/18/opinion/student-loans-cares-act.html', 'snippet': 'Millions of Americans were forgotten when Congress extended protection for student loan holders. I’m one of them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I have a secret shame: I am 38 years old and I carry enormous federal student loan debt, 14 years after finishing grad school. The CARES Act was supposed to help me. It didn’t.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/19/opinion/19webster/19webster-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/19/opinion/19webster/19webster-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/19/opinion/19webster/19webster-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/19/opinion/19webster/19webster-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/19/opinion/19webster/19webster-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/03/19/opinion/19webster/19webster-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/19/opinion/19webster/19webster-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'I’ve Spent $60,000 to Pay Back Student Loans and Owe More Than Before I Began', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drowning in Student Loan Debt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Student Loans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Biden, Joseph R Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pell Grants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus Aid, Relief, and Economic Security Act (2020)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-18T19:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Molly Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Molly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/65f891c7-b7dc-5ae6-88b9-2757806f6408', 'word_count': 1062, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65f891c7-b7dc-5ae6-88b9-2757806f6408'}, {'abstract': 'A very promising mental health experiment is taking shape in Oregon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/18/opinion/oregon-psychedelic-therapy.html', 'snippet': 'A very promising mental health experiment is taking shape in Oregon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Kate Brown of Oregon announced the members of the state’s newly formed Psilocybin Advisory Board this week.  Why does Oregon need an official board to offer advice about the active ingredient in magic mushrooms, you ask? Because Oregon is about to become the first state in the country to try to build a support infrastructure through which psychedelic mushrooms can be woven into everyday life. This framework is different from what we’ve seen before: not legalization, not medicalization, but therapeutic use, in licensed facilities, under the guidance of professionals trained to guide psychedelic experiences. Whoa.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/21/opinion/18klein/18klein-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 431, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/21/opinion/18klein/18klein-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 431}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/21/opinion/18klein/18klein-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 736, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/21/opinion/18klein/18kle