', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ezra Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ezra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/525c3e3c-2033-5894-b0d9-5566573519b0', 'word_count': 2326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/525c3e3c-2033-5894-b0d9-5566573519b0'}, {'abstract': 'Oscar Wilde meets Instagram in a slick, shrewd and screen-filled update, the filmed collaboration by five British theaters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/18/theater/review-picture-of-dorian-gray.html', 'snippet': 'Oscar Wilde meets Instagram in a slick, shrewd and screen-filled update, the filmed collaboration by five British theaters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of the Olympus-style pantheon of dead writers toasting with whiskey and Benzedrine in the heavens, Oscar Wilde, I’m willing to bet, would have the most Insta followers. C’mon, the guy had style.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/19/arts/18doriangray-3/18doriangray-3-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/19/arts/18doriangray-3/18doriangray-3-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/19/arts/18doriangray-3/18doriangray-3-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/19/arts/18doriangray-3/18doriangray-3-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/19/arts/18doriangray-3/18doriangray-3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/03/19/arts/18doriangray-3/18doriangray-3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/19/arts/18doriangray-3/18doriangray-3-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: A Selfie’s in the Picture for This ‘Dorian Gray’', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Pick', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'All the World’s a Screen, So You’d Better Be Beautiful', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings, Downloads and Streaming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Picture of Dorian Gray (Play)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barn Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Filloux-Bennett, Henry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harvey, Tamara', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilde, Oscar', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Picture of Dorian Gray (Book)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-18T19:07:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maya Phillips', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Phillips', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b812c054-8aad-5f8f-88b1-a2a546dc6241', 'word_count': 901, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b812c054-8aad-5f8f-88b1-a2a546dc6241'}, {'abstract': 'A show drawn primarily from the museum’s collection and archives tells the story of its relationship with this early favorite.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/18/arts/design/alexander-calder-moma.html', 'snippet': 'A show drawn primarily from the museum’s collection and archives tells the story of its relationship with this early favorite.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Where the Museum of Modern Art is concerned, the sculptor Alexander Calder was something like its American Picasso. Both were the precocious children of academically trained artists. Both were shaped by their own life-changing encounter, three decades apart, with the Parisian avant-garde. The Modern exhibited both of them early and often and acquired their work in some abundance — although of course, at MoMA, no other artist comes close to Picasso’s numbers. Much of each artist’s success was entwined with the museum’s; to some degree both were part of the MoMA brand, if in very different ways. For one thing, Calder has been M.I.A. for a while.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/18/arts/18calder-review-6/18calder-review-6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/18/arts/18calder-review-6/18calder-review-6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 750}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/18/arts/18calder-review-6/18calder-review-6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/18/arts