Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Draper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Draper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/78b74d5e-e067-5503-ba52-9ff3acc0348e', 'word_count': 1407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78b74d5e-e067-5503-ba52-9ff3acc0348e'}, {'abstract': 'They escaped traumatic circumstances in Myanmar and now live in harsh conditions. But refugees are creating murals drawn from their flourishing cultural traditions, reborn in Bangladesh camps.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/19/arts/design/rohingya-survivors-art-bangladesh.html', 'snippet': 'They escaped traumatic circumstances in Myanmar and now live in harsh conditions. But refugees are creating murals drawn from their flourishing cultural traditions, reborn in Bangladesh camps.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before he fled Myanmar in 2017, a witness to unspeakable horrors in his Rohingya village, Mohammed Nur would produce art in hiding, drawing on napkins and trash with bits of charcoal. Art, poetry readings and a university education were among many aspects of life that were not allowed for Rohingya Muslims like himself.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/21/arts/21rohingya-arts-9/merlin_181418748_4daa3bbf-12b3-4adc-a7ae-323e7d18551c-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/21/arts/21rohingya-arts-9/merlin_181418748_4daa3bbf-12b3-4adc-a7ae-323e7d18551c-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/21/arts/21rohingya-arts-9/merlin_181418748_4daa3bbf-12b3-4adc-a7ae-323e7d185