name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarantine (Life and Culture)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Catskills (NYS Area)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Content Type: Personal Profile', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-19T12:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Kurutz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kurutz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3476a956-99b7-5402-9938-a45969ff5ec1', 'word_count': 908, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3476a956-99b7-5402-9938-a45969ff5ec1'}, {'abstract': 'For Loren Daye, stripping a space back to its most essential elements is an aesthetic practice to live by.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/19/t-magazine/loren-daye-studio-design.html', 'snippet': 'For Loren Daye, stripping a space back to its most essential elements is an aesthetic practice to live by.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FROM THE BEGINNING, the interior designer Loren Daye appreciated the invisible. “I love anonymity and seeing without being seen,” she says. “The door you don’t notice until it’s open, the building you never realized was there, the quiet person at the party.” Growing up in Bowling Green, Ohio, she often hung out in her parents’ closets. After her father, a professor of Buddhist philosophy, was hired by the University of East Anglia, the family moved to its concrete campus in Norwich, England, where, in the attic of their new home, Daye transformed a giant cupboard into a reading room. From there, she taught herself about Denys Lasdun, the Brutalist English architect who in the 1960s designed the iconic, tiered postwar structures that surrounded their apartment.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '64', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/19/t-magazine/19tmag-lorendaye-slide-BHPN/19tmag-lorendaye-slide-BHPN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/19/t-magazine/19tmag-lorendaye-slide-BHPN/19tmag-lorendaye-slide-BHPN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/19/t-magazine/19tmag-lorendaye-slide-BHPN/19tmag-lorendaye-slide-BHPN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/19/t-magazine/19tmag-lorendaye-slide-BHPN/19tmag-lorendaye-slide-BHPN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1232, 'width': 1643, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/19/t-magazine/19tmag-lorendaye-slide-BHPN/19tmag-lorendaye-slide-BHPN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/03/19/t-magazine/19tmag-lorendaye-slide-BHPN/19tmag-lorendaye-slide-BHPN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/19/t-magazine/19tmag-lorendaye-slide-BHPN/19tmag-lorendaye-slide-BHPN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Designer Who Finds Beauty in Austerity', 'kicker': 'In Studio', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Disappearing Place', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Content Type: Personal Profile', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Clinton Hill (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daye, Loren', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-19T12:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kurt Soller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kurt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bf1b968-4c85-5d65-8959-fff78bf0f43e', 'word_count': 1107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bf1b968-4c85-5d65-8959-fff78bf0f43e'}, {'abstract': 'Junior bankers at Goldman Sachs raise pointed questions about pay, hours and working conditions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/19/business/dealbook/goldman-sachs-work-hours.html', 'snippet': 'Junior bankers at Goldman Sachs raise pointed questions about pay, hours and working conditions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A group of 13 disgruntled first-year analysts at Goldman Sachs made waves this week by assembling a professional-looking presentation in the company style about their experiences at the investment bank. The resulting “Working Conditions Survey” (sample size: 13) said that the junior bankers worked an average of around 100 hours per week, with most saying that they considered themselves victims of workplace abuse.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/19/business/19db-goldmanhq/merlin_149384421_599a22ba-b2e9-449b-9687-942137df70ba-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge