_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee8c9b1c-e3d9-5b36-84b6-cf8de247b5d2', 'word_count': 1261, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee8c9b1c-e3d9-5b36-84b6-cf8de247b5d2'}, {'abstract': '“Ulysses” and “The Waste Land” appeared in 1922. But three years later, masterworks by Virginia Woolf, F. Scott Fitzgerald, Ernest Hemingway and John Dos Passos gave the movement its signature forms — and influence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/20/books/review/woolf-dos-passos-hemingway-fitzgerald-1925-manhattan-transfer-great-gatsby-mrs-dalloway.html', 'snippet': '“Ulysses” and “The Waste Land” appeared in 1922. But three years later, masterworks by Virginia Woolf, F. Scott Fitzgerald, Ernest Hemingway and John Dos Passos gave the movement its signature forms — and influence.', 'lead_paragraph': '“An illiterate, underbred book it seems to me: the book of a self-taught working man, & we all know how distressing they are, how egotistic, insistent, raw, striking & ultimately nauseating.” So goes Virginia Woolf’s well-known complaint about “Ulysses,” scribbled into her diary before she had finished reading it. Her disparagement is catnip to those many critics who like to view “Mrs. Dalloway” — that other uber-famous, if more lapidary, modernist novel that spans the course of a single day — as Woolf’s rejoinder to Joyce. More than that, though, it tells us something important about our literary history. Nineteen twenty-two, the year of “Ulysses,” may well be ground zero for the explosion of modernism in literature. But the resultant shock wave is better captured by another year: 1925, that of “Mrs. Dalloway” and several other works, all now in the spotlight in 2021, as they emerge from under copyright.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/28/books/review/28Libman/28Libman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 565, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/28/books/review/28Libman/28Libman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 565}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/28/books/review/28Libman/28Libman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 964, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/28/books/review/28Libman/28Libman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1734, 'width': 1842, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/28/books/review/28Libman/28Libman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/03/28/books/review/28Libman/28Libman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/28/books/review/28Libman/28Libman-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Was 1925 Literary Modernism’s Most Important Year?', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Year Modernism Found Its Voice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Mrs Dalloway (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woolf, Virginia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hemingway, Ernest', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'In Our Time (Book)'