eral Reserve System', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-22T23:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4338591f-fda2-54c8-8dc8-00507237811d', 'word_count': 887, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4338591f-fda2-54c8-8dc8-00507237811d'}, {'abstract': 'The festival in Austin, which was canceled last year, returned online with more international performers and music forged during the pandemic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/22/arts/music/sxsw-music-festival.html', 'snippet': 'The festival in Austin, which was canceled last year, returned online with more international performers and music forged during the pandemic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'South by Southwest 2020 was abruptly canceled last March as the reality of Covid-19 set in. This year, it returned March 16-20 as SXSW Online, viewed remotely and downscaled to the size of a screen. Its music festival offered prerecorded virtual showcases — from studios, clubs, living rooms, backyards, city streets and odder places — for about 280 bands rather than the 1,000-plus of recent years. Though some showcases vanished after they were webcast, conference attendees can rerun many of them until April 18, and with luck the performances will make their way to a wider public afterward. For once, it’s possible to see nearly all the music at SXSW.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/22/arts/22sxsw-music7/22sxsw-music7-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 285, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'imag