, 'snippet': 'Who knew an animated series about misanthropic space aliens could feel so relevant? Mike McMahan and Justin Roiland explained ahead of Season 2 why it isn’t their fault.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Half of them hate it, half of them love it. But nobody knows more about American pop culture than the aliens in “Solar Opposites,” who have crash-landed in suburbia and absorbed the culture as voraciously as Daryl Hannah’s TV-addicted mermaid in “Splash.” Justin Roiland, who created the animated series for Hulu with Mike McMahan, believes he would do the same thing if he found himself on their home, on the utopian planet of Shlorp.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/25/arts/25SOLAR1/25SOLAR1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 409, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/25/arts/25SOLAR1/25SOLAR1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 409}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/25/arts/25SOLAR1/25SOLAR1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 697, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/25/arts/25SOLAR1/25SOLAR1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1395, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/25/arts/25SOLAR1/25SOLAR1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/03/25/arts/25SOLAR1/25SOLAR1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/25/arts/25SOLAR1/25SOLAR1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The ‘Solar Opposites’ Creators Apologize for Their Clairvoyance', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sitcom Format + Sci-Fi + Satire = Truth?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Content Type: Personal Profile', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roiland, Justin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McMahan, Mike (Screenwriter)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Solar Opposites (TV Program)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hulu.com', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Rick and Morty (TV Program)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-03-25T11:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Tobias', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tobias', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e23e97f-945d-5e3b-9fcd-2ddc2390624d', 'word_count': 1701, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e23e97f-945d-5e3b-9fcd-2ddc2390624d'}, {'abstract': 'In this suspense thriller set in the 1930s, Judi Dench and Eddie Izzard are stalwart Brits at a sinister girls’ school in England.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/25/movies/six-minutes-to-midnight-review.html', 'snippet': 'In this suspense thriller set in the 1930s, Judi Dench and Eddie Izzard are stalwart B