ocument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Landler and Stephen Castle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Castle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc694106-b979-525c-bf86-85ef88457ead', 'word_count': 1143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc694106-b979-525c-bf86-85ef88457ead'}, {'abstract': 'Fausto Melotti’s ceramic vases; Susan Weil’s paintings and collages; and mail art in “Poema Colectivo Revolución.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/31/arts/design/3-art-gallery-shows-to-see-right-now.html', 'snippet': 'Fausto Melotti’s ceramic vases; Susan Weil’s paintings and collages; and mail art in “Poema Colectivo Revolución.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Milanese sculptor Fausto Melotti (1901-86) remains best known for abstract, lightweight constructions of metal filaments, from which he suspended thinly pounded sheets of brass and gold. (His friend, the writer Italo Calvino, called them “a score of weightless ideograms,” and used them as an inspiration for his novel “Invisible Cities.”) New Yorkers saw many of these rather outmoded sculptures at Hauser & Wirth in 2018, and another one, comprising seven strips of brass entangled in looping wires, is on view now at Barbara Mathes Gallery in the Upper East Side, sitting over the mantelpiece. But Melotti also worked in ceramics — and as everyone from MoMA’s curators to Brad Pitt is now rediscovering the importance of pottery, this artist’s colorful and crooked work in clay outshines the metal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url'